{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head 79da95485d131ce20402ba2b1f17e3161279c0f4, reporting artifact 25af892556b0614a4583d9cd082dc1afbb564f8a and the three named exact-path companions, under implementation request b274f991 and review request eb68a14b.\n\nArchitecture: layer 2, Kernel (internal/intent and internal/kernel), deliberately tightens malformed-envelope acceptance. The same head updates docs/reference/architecture.md to require ordered element equality, including duplicates; its candidate artifact e5829151 rests directly on the implementing envelope artifact. The kernel still carries opaque signed references without Workroom semantics. No other layer contract changes.\n\nSecurity: slices.Equal compares count and whole ordered elements; nil and an empty list remain equal, and duplicate multiplicity remains significant. Empty and NUL-bearing signed references remain invalid. The actual defect requires a valid sequencer signature over an envelope whose actor signature remains valid; it does not bypass either signature or manufacture decoded causal data. Both full/delta verification and checkpoint payload validation use the corrected comparator. Signatures, object identity, target checks, payload bounds and fallback behavior are unchanged; no history is rewritten or normalized. The published-checkpoint and frontier regressions exercise the real refusal paths, with direct errors and reader counters distinguishing restoration from fallback. I found no unresolved security defect. This is assigned repair of an explicit correctness outcome, not adoption through the satisfied authority-bearing decision-request path. The author explicitly reconciled historical staleness at the current source head, including correction aff5fb7a; no new governing decision or conditions are introduced.\n\nCompatibility: the change refuses a malformed empty trailer previously accepted. The planner's signed intake evidence covers only the four named reachable histories at its recorde